  "nl_text": "The database schema named \"schemabench_0217\" consists of 12 tables. The database is designed to manage various aspects of a school system, including administrative users, school information, student and teacher profiles, book information, category, book-category relationships, author, book-author relationships, key words, book availability, and application processes.\nThe 'admin' table is used to store administrative user information, including unique identifiers, usernames, and passwords. The 'school' table stores information about each school, including unique identifiers, names, emails, principal's first and last names, city, address, and phone numbers. The 'user' table contains information about all users, including unique identifiers, usernames, passwords, first and last names, birth dates, statuses, borrow counts, roles, school names, and reservation counts. The 'book' table contains information about all books, including ISBNs, titles, summaries, publishers, page numbers, languages, and image URLs.\nThe 'category' and 'book_category' tables are used to manage book categories, with the 'book_category' table linking books to their categories. The 'author' and 'book_author' tables manage book authors, with the 'book_author' table linking books to their authors. The 'key_words' table stores keywords related to books, and the 'stores' table keeps track of the availability of books in different schools.\nThe 'applications' table manages book applications, with information about the user who applied, the book, start and expiration dates, and the status of the application. The 'review' table allows users to leave reviews for books, with information about the book, user, evaluation, like scale, approval status, and review date.\nAll these tables are interconnected, with foreign keys ensuring referential integrity and maintaining the consistency of the data. For instance, the 'user' table references the 'school' table, the 'book' table is referenced by several other tables, and so on. The database schema is designed to be flexible and scalable, allowing for easy addition, modification, and deletion of data as needed.",
  "gold_question": "School Library Management System \n\n1) Background / Context\n\nThe project involves developing a School Library Management System for public schools to streamline library operations. The system will facilitate the management of library resources, ensuring that technological needs for efficient library management are met.\n\n2) Objectives\n\n- Establish a centralized system for managing school libraries.\n- Enable efficient registration and administration of books and user information.\n- Facilitate borrowing, returning, and reserving books.\n- Allow user review and rating of books.\n\n3) Stakeholders & Users\n\n- Network School Library Administrator: Manages schools, appoints library operators, and handles database management.\n- School Library Operator: Manages book data, user approvals, and supervises borrowing activities.\n- Students and Teachers: Register, view, and borrow books, make reservations, and submit reviews.\n\n4) Functional Descriptions\n\n- School Management: Register each school with details including name, contact information, and key personnel.\n- Book Management: Maintain comprehensive details about each book—title, publisher, authors, category, etc.\n- User Roles & Authentication:\n  - Admins register and approve library operators.\n  - Operators manage library operations and user interactions.\n  - Students and teachers register, view, borrow, and review books.\n- Borrowing & Reservations:\n  - Students: Borrow up to 2 books/week.\n  - Teachers: Borrow up to 1 book/week.\n  - Reservations are limited by borrowing capacity and expire after 1 week if not fulfilled.\n- Reviews & Ratings: Users can submit reviews and ratings which need approval from library operators.\n\n5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Ensure secure user authentication and authorization processes.\n- Support robust data management, including backups and restorations.\n- Facilitate user-friendly interface for all functionalities.\n- Scale to manage multiple public school libraries efficiently.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_deepseek/0217.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0217\nCREATE TABLE `admin` (\n  `admin_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `username` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`admin_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `school` (\n  `school_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `principal_first_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `principal_last_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `city` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `address` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `phone_number` varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`school_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `user` (\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `username` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `first_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `last_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `birth_date` date NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `borrow_count` int(11) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  `role` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `school_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `reservation_count` int(11) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`user_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `user_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`school_id`) REFERENCES `school` (`school_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `book` (\n  `book_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `isbn` varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  `title` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `summary` text NOT NULL,\n  `publisher` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `page_count` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `language` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `image_url` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`book_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `category` (\n  `category_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`category_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `book_category` (\n  `book_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `category_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`book_id`, `category_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `book_category_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`book_id`) REFERENCES `book` (`book_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `book_category_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`category_id`) REFERENCES `category` (`category_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `author` (\n  `author_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`author_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `book_author` (\n  `book_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `author_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`book_id`, `author_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `book_author_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`book_id`) REFERENCES `book` (`book_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `book_author_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`author_id`) REFERENCES `author` (`author_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `key_words` (\n  `keyword_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `keyword` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`keyword_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `stores` (\n  `store_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `book_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `school_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `availability` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`store_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `stores_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`book_id`) REFERENCES `book` (`book_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `stores_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`school_id`) REFERENCES `school` (`school_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `applications` (\n  `application_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `book_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `start_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `expiration_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`application_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `applications_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `user` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `applications_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`book_id`) REFERENCES `book` (`book_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `review` (\n  `review_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `book_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `evaluation` text NOT NULL,\n  `like_scale` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `approval_status` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `review_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`review_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `review_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`book_id`) REFERENCES `book` (`book_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `review_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `user` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
